<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <RootNamespace>Broiler.VM.Composition.JavaScript.Cli</RootNamespace>
    <AssemblyName>Broiler.VM.Composition.JavaScript.Cli</AssemblyName>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>

    <!-- NOT PACKABLE, AND THAT IS THE ONE PROPERTY OF THIS ROOT A READER IS MOST LIKELY TO GET
         WRONG. This is the end-user host: point it at a .js file and it compiles, verifies and
         runs it. It is therefore the first root in this checkout that a person would expect to be
         shipped - and it is not, because `docs/compositions.md` section 1 advertises nothing and
         because a tool advertised as a JavaScript host has to be able to run JavaScript. This one
         admits no function, no object and no string value, so it runs almost none. Advertising it
         would be the untruthful support claim the core roadmap makes a stop condition. -->
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>

    <!-- POINTER TYPES, BECAUSE THE MAPPER INSTALL NAMES ONE. The hook this root fills is
         `JsNativePage.Mapper`, and the page it hands back carries a function pointer to the
         entry of the mapped region. Converting that pointer to an `nint` is a pointer
         conversion, so the file holding the install needs unsafe blocks admitted. The
         slice-compiler root says the same thing about the same install; this root needs it for
         no other reason, and no other file here takes an address. -->
    <AllowUnsafeBlocks>true</AllowUnsafeBlocks>

    <PublishTrimmed>true</PublishTrimmed>
    <TrimMode>full</TrimMode>
    <InvariantGlobalization>true</InvariantGlobalization>
    <UseSystemResourceKeys>true</UseSystemResourceKeys>

    <TrimmerSingleWarn>false</TrimmerSingleWarn>
    <TreatWarningsAsErrors>true</TreatWarningsAsErrors>
    <IsAotCompatible>true</IsAotCompatible>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    THE `narrow-runtime-compiler` COMPOSITION, AND THE FIRST ROOT THAT EARNS THAT LABEL.

    Section 15 of the profile roadmap defines the label as a composition carrying "tokenizer,
    static semantics, and lowering for a named restricted surface", and `docs/compositions.md`
    recorded until now that no root here held it: the slice-compiler root is "only shaped like
    one", because what it lowers is a programmatic builder rather than SOURCE, and "there is no
    source surface until JS-3b writes the tokenizer and the static semantics".

    JS-3b wrote them. What was missing after that was a composition that takes source from OUTSIDE
    the image - a file a person names on a command line - and turns it into a running program
    inside the published application. That is this root, and it is the subject of JS-3b's
    publish-and-run gate.

    WHAT IT DELIBERATELY DOES NOT CARRY. No census, no corpus producer, no cross-profile checks,
    no conformance harness, no fuzz mutator, no soak. Every sibling root here carries some of
    that, forced by rules A11 and A12 leaving such code nowhere else - and each of them says so.
    This root is the one whose closure has no such excuse to make: it reads a file, compiles it,
    verifies it, runs it, and reports. A reader comparing its closure against the label's row in
    section 15 should find nothing to explain away.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Broiler.VM.Abstractions\Broiler.VM.Abstractions.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Broiler.VM.Binary\Broiler.VM.Binary.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Broiler.VM.Runtime\Broiler.VM.Runtime.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Broiler.VM.Profile.JavaScript\Broiler.VM.Profile.JavaScript.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Broiler.VM.Profile.JavaScript.Compiler\Broiler.VM.Profile.JavaScript.Compiler.csproj" />

    <!-- THE ARMING PATH, RESTORED TO THIS ROOT ON 2026-09-23. The native-backend option is one
         this host's own help text describes at length, and it promises that a program compiled
         under this process's calling convention "is armed and executed here". The arming path
         left the profile assembly on 2026-09-18 and was rearmed for the slice-compiler root
         alone, so from that day the option refused every artifact it was given -
         ProfileFault/UnsatisfiedHostAssumption - while the help text went on promising a run.
         Naming the assembly here is what puts it back in this image's closure; the install that
         fills `JsNativePage.Mapper` is in Program.cs, and docs/compositions.md's
         native-execution cell for this row moves with both.

         This is the one sibling this root links that is not part of "read a file, compile it,
         verify it, run it, report". It is part of RUN: the option that names a backend has to
         reach an image that can arm one, and a host that carries the encoders and not the page
         is a host whose only honest answer to that option is a refusal. -->
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Broiler.VM.Profile.MachineCode\Broiler.VM.Profile.MachineCode.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
